Q: How can I place text boxes on the same line while allowing responsive widths longer than the browser default? A: <p>I think you should change the width of your <code>flexbox</code> to 100%, and also, set your <code>flex-direction:row</code>, so that it avoids turning into two columns by default on most laptop-sized and PC-sized screens. Then, I would set the width of your elements to what you need, but as a percentage, and associate the labels above with the inputs or make the input titles into placeholders. Here is an example:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html lang="en"&gt;
&lt;head&gt;
    &lt;meta charset="UTF-8"&gt;
    &lt;meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0"&gt;
    &lt;title&gt;Example&lt;/title&gt;
    &lt;style&gt;
    #main-div{
    width:"100%",
    flex-direction:row,
    }
    &lt;/style&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;
&lt;body&gt;
    &lt;div id="main-div"&gt;
    &lt;input style="width:60%; margin-bottom:20px;" placeholder="Name"/&gt;
    &lt;input style="width:30%" placeholder="Phone"/&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
